      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-319",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "ParserResource.writeFlickerFeed() builds its outbound request with `new URL(\"http://api.flickr.com/services/rest/?method=flickr.photos.getRecent&api_key=221160312e1c22ec60ecf336951b0e77&format=json&nojsoncallback=1&per_page=20\")` \u2014 the Flickr API key is both hardcoded in source and transmitted in cleartext over plain HTTP (not HTTPS), so there is no encryption-in-transit control protecting this credential from network eavesdroppers/MITM, and the key is permanently exposed in version control.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly against the real source: ParserResource.java line 53 (method writeFlickerFeed, called from the JAX-RS @GET @Path(\"/parser\") handler doGet() at line 44-49) constructs `new URL(\"http://api.flickr.com/services/rest/?method=flickr.photos.getRecent&api_key=221160312e1c22ec60ecf336951b0e77&format=json&nojsoncallback=1&per_page=20\")` and immediately calls `url.openStream()` on it. The URL uses plain `http://` (not https), so the hardcoded API key `221160312e1c22ec60ecf336951b0e77` travels in cleartext to api.flickr.com and is also permanently committed in source/version control. Graph query confirms: the CpgCall at ParserResource.java:53 has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=3, and `doGet`/the StreamingOutput.write lambda are registered EntryPoints with exposure=\"reachable\", kind=\"handler\" \u2014 i.e. this is a real, exposed REST endpoint path, not dead code. This is a genuine demo app (org.eclipse.parsson.demos.rest), so the exposed key only grants quota-limited access to Flickr's public read-only getRecent API (not an internal or high-value secret), which caps real-world impact \u2014 hence MEDIUM rather than HIGH/CRITICAL severity."
          },
