        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-294",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "TwitterObjectSearch.getSearchStream() builds the OAuth 1.0a signature parameters with a hardcoded, constant `oauth_nonce` value (`\"4b25256957d75b6370f33a4501dc5e7e\"`, marked with a `// TODO` comment) instead of generating a fresh random nonce per request. The OAuth nonce exists specifically to prevent replay of captured signed requests; because it never changes, the anti-replay control is effectively absent/disabled \u2014 any captured request (with its timestamp window) could be replayed.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Source inspection confirms the claim: TwitterObjectSearch.getSearchStream() builds the OAuth 1.0a parameter map with a literal, hardcoded oauth_nonce string instead of a per-request random value, flagged with a \"// TODO\" comment -- i.e. the developer never implemented nonce generation. The oauth_timestamp is computed fresh each call (System.currentTimeMillis()/1000) but oauth_nonce is a constant, so the anti-replay nonce component of the OAuth 1.0a signature scheme is effectively disabled: every signed request this code produces reuses the same nonce. The call is inside TwitterObjectSearch.main (the only EntryPoint in this file, kind=handler, exposure=reachable), so it is unconditionally reached whenever the demo runs -- reachability is not in doubt. This matches CWE-294 (Authentication Bypass by Capture-replay): a captured Authorization header/signature could be more easily replayed since the nonce never varies to disambiguate requests sharing a timestamp second. Severity is tempered because this is example/demo code (parsson's Twitter demo) shipped with blank placeholder credentials in twitterconfig.properties, not a production authentication path serving real users -- the weakness is real but the blast radius is limited to whoever adapts this sample code for their own Twitter API use."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "demos/twitter/src/main/java/org/eclipse/parsson/demos/twitter/TwitterObjectSearch.java",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
