        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-598",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "FacebookObjectSearch.getSearchStream() appends the Facebook Graph API `access_token` directly into the request URL's query string (`searchUrl + accessToken` where searchUrl ends in `...&access_token=`) instead of sending it via a secure Authorization header (contrast with the Twitter demo in the same repo, which does use an Authorization header for its OAuth token). Secrets placed in a URL query string are commonly leaked via server access logs, proxy logs, browser history, and the Referer header \u2014 this is the standard missing control for credential transmission even though the request itself is over HTTPS.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly in source: FacebookObjectSearch.getSearchStream() (lines 67-68) builds `searchUrl = \".../search?q=tamil&type=post&access_token=\"` then does `new URL(searchUrl+accessToken)`, placing the Graph API access token in the request URL's query string with no Authorization header set anywhere in the method. The sibling TwitterObjectSearch.getSearchStream() in the same repo (lines 126-146) correctly sends its OAuth credential via `con.addRequestProperty(\"Authorization\", ...)`, confirming the contrast is real and the secure alternative pattern exists in-repo. Graph query confirms the URL-construction call at line 68 is reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=3, reached via main() -> getSearchStream() (EntryPoint query shows FacebookObjectSearch.main is a reachable handler entry point), so this is live, reachable code, not dead code."
          },
