        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-330",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "In demos/twitter/src/main/java/.../TwitterObjectSearch.java, getSearchStream() builds the OAuth 1.0a request-signing parameter map with a hardcoded 'oauth_nonce' value (\"4b25256957d75b6370f33a4501dc5e7e\") marked with a '// TODO' comment, instead of generating a fresh random nonce per request. The nonce's security purpose is to make each signed request unique and prevent replay; a constant nonce defeats that protection. The TODO indicates this was a known-incomplete implementation left in place. Both TwitterObjectSearch.main and TwitterStreamSearch.main are EntryPoint methods that reach this code (reachable_from_entry=true on the call site).\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly against real source: TwitterObjectSearch.getSearchStream() builds the OAuth1.0a parameter TreeMap and the 'oauth_nonce' value is the literal hardcoded string \"4b25256957d75b6370f33a4501dc5e7e\" with a \"// TODO\" comment immediately preceding it, rather than a randomly generated nonce. This map is then used to build the HMAC-SHA1 signature base string and the final \"Authorization\" OAuth header sent to the real Twitter search API endpoint \u2014 so the constant nonce is actually used in the live signing/request path, not just dead code. Reachability: graph confirms two EntryPoint/handler nodes (TwitterObjectSearch.main and TwitterStreamSearch.main, both exposure=\"reachable\") exist, and TwitterStreamSearch.main (line 44) directly calls TwitterObjectSearch.getSearchStream(), which itself is called from TwitterObjectSearch.main (line 47) \u2014 matching the source. The CpgCall nodes for the TreeMap construction/assignment/alloc containing this code all have reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=3. This is a genuine, known-incomplete (TODO-flagged) weakening of OAuth replay protection: CWE-330 (reuse of a fixed/insufficiently-random value where freshness is a security property)."
          },
