        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1104",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Test-scope dependencies hamcrest-core (1.3, released 2012) and junit (4.13.2, legacy JUnit4 branch) are old/EOL-track versions declared in the project manifest; low exploitability since they are test-only and not shipped in the runtime artifact, but worth a version bump as part of dependency hygiene.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from both the graph and the real source. Cypher query against scan_id 04a8a41c4ca2bbfdbea3f8f6ce37bbd00afbe802 returns exactly the two rows the analyst cited: hamcrest-core 1.3 and junit 4.13.2. Reading the actual manifests confirms both are declared with Maven scope=test everywhere they are used as direct dependencies: root pom.xml dependencyManagement pins junit to 4.13.2 (lines 424-428) and hamcrest-core to 1.3 with explicit <scope>test</scope> (lines 429-434); impl/pom.xml (lines 99-103) declares the junit dependency with <scope>test</scope>; demos/customprovider-jdk9/pom.xml (lines 81-85) declares hamcrest-core with <scope>test</scope>. Maven excludes test-scope dependencies from the packaged/runtime artifact, so these jars are not shipped in production, matching the analyst's low-exploitability rationale. Additionally, junit 4.13.2 already contains the fix for the one notable JUnit4 CVE (CVE-2020-15250, insecure temp-file permissions in TemporaryFolder, fixed in 4.13.1), so there isn't even a live CVE affecting this exact pinned version; hamcrest-core 1.3 has no significant known CVEs either. exploit_search returned \"corpus unavailable: not indexed\" for both components, so no weaponized-exploit evidence either way -- this doesn't change the verdict since the claim was never about an active exploit, only dependency hygiene. Overall: the lead's factual claims are accurate and verified against source/graph; it is a legitimate but low-severity, non-exploitable hygiene finding, exactly as the analyst scoped it."
          },
